\title{Certified Convergence and a Sharp Existence Threshold for
Exp--Log (EML) Fixed-Point Iteration}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}
\begin{abstract}
We study the single-operator \emph{exp--log} (EML) map
$\eml(x) = e^{a}\log(bx+c)$ as a discrete dynamical system and establish four
groups of results. (i)~On any closed interval $[\ell,h]$ on which $f$ is
self-mapping with $|f'|\le\rho<1$, the map has a unique fixed point $x^*$ and
the Picard iteration $x_{n+1}=f(x_n)$ converges to it from every starting point.
(ii)~The iteration satisfies the explicit a~priori bound
$|x_n-x^*|\le |x_1-x_0|\,\rho^n/(1-\rho)\to0$, a computable $O(\rho^n)$
certificate. (iii)~For $b=1$ a fixed point exists in the natural domain $x+c>0$
if and only if $c\ge e^a(1-a)$; exactly on the threshold the fixed point
$x^*=e^a-c$ is neutral, $f'(x^*)=1$, so the existence frontier equals the
contraction frontier. (iv)~For $b>0$ the map is monotone and the endpoint orbits
bracket $x^*$ with width tending to $0$. A fully worked instance,
$f(x)=e\log(x+100)$ on $[0,20]$ with $\rho=1/30$, carries an end-to-end
certified error bound. A corollary falsifies the naive expectation that the box
$a\in(0,1),\,b=1,\,c\in(0,1)$ yields fixed points: $a=c=\tfrac12$ has none.
\end{abstract}

